WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.877 --> 00:00:02.955
An assembly station on a line.

2
00:00:03.648 --> 00:00:06.121
The operator lays a housing in the holder and

3
00:00:06.179 --> 00:00:09.104
puts a steel bracket and a plastic clip on it.

4
00:00:09.896 --> 00:00:13.147
Then screws go in, and the press presses in a bearing.

5
00:00:14.158 --> 00:00:18.094
On the left the bins of brackets, above them the nutrunner and the press.

6
00:00:19.134 --> 00:00:21.166
This happens hundreds of times a day,

7
00:00:21.340 --> 00:00:25.357
so even a tiny chance of error gives faulty products every week.

8
00:00:26.655 --> 00:00:31.147
The line builds two heaters: with a 6 millimetre bracket, or an 8.

9
00:00:31.881 --> 00:00:35.097
They look almost the same, and lie side by side.

10
00:00:36.132 --> 00:00:40.323
After an interruption the operator picks from the wrong bin: a mistake,

11
00:00:40.485 --> 00:00:42.308
the human action that goes wrong.

12
00:00:43.057 --> 00:00:45.309
Everyone makes mistakes now and then.

13
00:00:46.299 --> 00:00:50.978
If it moves on like this, the customer gets a defect: a faulty product.

14
00:00:51.717 --> 00:00:54.909
Shigeo Shingo said: mistakes happen, defects need not,

15
00:00:55.095 --> 00:00:56.999
if you catch the mistake in time.

16
00:00:57.960 --> 00:01:01.060
You count defects in ppm, parts per million.

17
00:01:01.807 --> 00:01:04.709
One in 5,000 is 200 ppm.

18
00:01:05.416 --> 00:01:08.027
Do not ask who did it, but how it could happen.

19
00:01:09.329 --> 00:01:11.175
Shingo’s answer was poka-yoke,

20
00:01:11.303 --> 00:01:13.683
Japanese for avoiding inadvertent errors:

21
00:01:13.846 --> 00:01:16.504
simple devices that make a mistake impossible,

22
00:01:16.620 --> 00:01:17.862
or catch it at once.

23
00:01:18.844 --> 00:01:22.234
Here that is pick-to-light: a lamp lights at the right bin,

24
00:01:22.431 --> 00:01:25.043
and a light beam sees whether the hand went in.

25
00:01:26.016 --> 00:01:29.800
If the hand goes into the wrong bin, the clamp stays shut.

26
00:01:30.591 --> 00:01:34.469
A stop is stronger than a warning: you miss a buzzer in a noisy hall.

27
00:01:35.449 --> 00:01:37.841
Better still: make the error impossible.

28
00:01:38.560 --> 00:01:43.274
The fixture puts the housing in place; its pin fits only the chamfered corner,

29
00:01:43.379 --> 00:01:45.515
so the wrong way round is impossible.

30
00:01:46.817 --> 00:01:49.140
If you cannot, detect the error and stop.

31
00:01:49.901 --> 00:01:53.094
An inductive sensor sees metal a few millimetres away,

32
00:01:53.210 --> 00:01:55.114
without touching: here the bracket.

33
00:01:56.180 --> 00:02:01.021
A photoelectric sensor works with light: the housing in the fixture breaks the beam.

34
00:02:02.005 --> 00:02:06.928
They report to the PLC: the small industrial computer that runs the machine.

35
00:02:08.219 --> 00:02:12.329
Before, one housing in 2,000 lacked one of its 8 screws.

36
00:02:13.044 --> 00:02:18.048
Now the nutrunner measures the torque and angle of every screw; the PLC counts.

37
00:02:19.089 --> 00:02:24.569
The interlock is a rule in the PLC: the next step only when every check is OK.

38
00:02:25.247 --> 00:02:28.718
Only at eight OKs does the clamp release the housing.

39
00:02:29.724 --> 00:02:30.920
An extra inspector?

40
00:02:31.645 --> 00:02:34.780
By eye you usually catch 80 to 90 percent.

41
00:02:35.546 --> 00:02:41.815
Of 400 ppm made, with 85 percent caught, 60 ppm still reach the customer.

42
00:02:42.845 --> 00:02:47.001
Watch for a false reject too: a good product the check turns down.

43
00:02:47.684 --> 00:02:51.666
Too loose lets defects through; too tight gives false rejects.

44
00:02:52.689 --> 00:02:57.367
If a camera rejects 3 percent good products, someone finds the bypass key.

45
00:02:58.039 --> 00:03:00.709
So ask how often each check is bypassed.

46
00:03:02.044 --> 00:03:04.714
Start from the defect: what is physically different?

47
00:03:05.472 --> 00:03:06.784
Here a plastic clip.

48
00:03:07.481 --> 00:03:10.918
An inductive sensor sees only the steel bracket behind it,

49
00:03:11.127 --> 00:03:13.101
so it always reports present.

50
00:03:14.174 --> 00:03:19.584
A capacitive sensor sees plastic and liquid too, but also reacts to damp and dirt.

51
00:03:20.667 --> 00:03:23.697
A diffuse sensor with background suppression fits here:

52
00:03:23.836 --> 00:03:26.414
it ignores anything beyond a set distance,

53
00:03:26.553 --> 00:03:28.353
just in front of the bracket.

54
00:03:29.681 --> 00:03:33.594
Force-displacement monitoring records the force over the press stroke,

55
00:03:33.814 --> 00:03:36.914
and tests the curve against windows from good parts.

56
00:03:37.917 --> 00:03:41.435
A missing bearing or a cracked housing gives a different curve.

57
00:03:42.141 --> 00:03:44.428
So the press judges the joint itself.

58
00:03:45.418 --> 00:03:50.573
Prove every check with a reference part for each failure mode: good, without a clip,

59
00:03:50.794 --> 00:03:53.035
and the borderline case: half-seated.

60
00:03:53.756 --> 00:03:55.892
Every bad part must be rejected.

61
00:03:57.224 --> 00:03:59.116
Where do you put a poka-yoke first?

62
00:03:59.867 --> 00:04:05.719
The PFMEA, the process failure mode and effects analysis, decides.

63
00:04:06.424 --> 00:04:11.184
Each failure mode scores 1 to 10 for severity, occurrence and detection.

64
00:04:12.208 --> 00:04:16.678
A table turns them into an action priority: high, medium or low.

65
00:04:17.353 --> 00:04:18.746
Severity comes first.

66
00:04:19.513 --> 00:04:22.415
Inspection by eye scores badly on detection,

67
00:04:22.567 --> 00:04:25.110
a validated sensor with an interlock well.

68
00:04:26.140 --> 00:04:29.275
An escape slips past every check, to the customer.

69
00:04:30.054 --> 00:04:37.009
1,000 ppm with 99.9 percent caught gives 1 ppm; by eye, 150.

70
00:04:38.308 --> 00:04:40.514
A check only counts while it works.

71
00:04:41.218 --> 00:04:44.991
So every shift a red rabbit runs through: a known bad part,

72
00:04:45.154 --> 00:04:48.591
often painted red; here the dark housing without a bearing.

73
00:04:49.593 --> 00:04:52.472
At six in the morning the press rejects it: good.

74
00:04:53.241 --> 00:04:56.910
At two in the afternoon it accepts it: the check has failed.

75
00:04:58.024 --> 00:05:02.017
Then the reaction plan starts: stop, repair, verify again,

76
00:05:02.215 --> 00:05:04.990
and contain everything since the last good check,

77
00:05:05.152 --> 00:05:07.137
because none of it was really checked.

78
00:05:08.194 --> 00:05:12.118
8 hours times 60 an hour: 480 parts.

79
00:05:12.819 --> 00:05:15.977
The check interval is also the size of every recall.
